ent dedups; normal resume re-fires nothing | replay same event twice; separately, resume | re-delivery returns `skipped`/`duplicate_idempotency_key`; resume produces no historical re-fire |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:78:> Hard-kill (no `agent_end`) is covered as a deterministic-boundary golden in `turn-derivation.test.ts`: open turn left open, tolerated as a no-op on reattach (no duplication of the recorded prefix).\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:92:| TC-4.1 | Function resolves (provider,model) via registry, returns text | fake registry + complete returning text | returns `{ok:true,text}` for a single-turn message list |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:93:| TC-4.2 | Two kinds → different (provider,model) both route in one session | two assignments, two fakes | each call routes by its keys; both succeed |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:101:| TC-4.5 | Captured thread's queued derivation invokes fn + persists outcome | captured thread; fakes (one text, one failure); `drainSettled` | at least one ready derived form + one classified failure, both queryable via inspect/health |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:118:| TC-5.6 | Incomplete/unknown assignment fails loud at init | missing kind / unknown prompt / placeholder | construction throws actionable error; no placeholder masks it |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:124:| TC-6.1 | Each corpus replays to a thread whose read-back matches the fixture | chatty, tool-heavy, parallel, error, aborted corpora | read-back (events/messages/turns) matches fixture expectation per corpus |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:128:### `test/fixtures/corpus.test.ts` *(risk: fixture validity)*\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:138:Each chunk: write skeletons (structured-result stubs for services/adapters/handlers; `NotImplementedError` for pure algorithms) → Red (tests written, failing for the right reason — never asserting on `NotImplementedError`) → Green (implement to pass). Exit each chunk on `green-verify`.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:142:- **Skeleton:** all interface exports (`tech-design.md` → Interface Definitions); `index.ts` hook registration with stubbed handlers; `state.ts` complete (plain data, no stub).\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:143:- **Red:** fixture-invariant tests (corpus shapes, synthetic coherence, temp-thread create+reopen); extension-loads smoke; package verification scripts/config present.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:144:- **Green:** fixtures + loaders + temp-thread factory implemented; extension registers hooks; `red-verify`/`verify`/`green-verify`/`verify-all` scripts run.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:149:- **Skeleton:** `instance.ts`, `thread-resolution.ts`, `picker.ts` as structured-result stubs.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:156:- **Skeleton:** `map-message.ts`, `turn-accumulator.ts`, `idempotency.ts` (`NotImplementedError`); `converter.ts` (structured result).\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:164:- **Red:** TC-6.1–6.3 + fixture-validity risk test, failing.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/test-plan.md:201:No suite is skipped on auth availability — the closed-loop test (TC-4.5) uses a deterministic `ModelCall` fake, not a live provider, so it always runs.\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.test.ts:3:import { fakeModelCallFailure, fakeModelCallRouter, fakeModelCallText } from \"./model-call.js\";\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.test.ts:11:describe(\"model-call fakes\", () => {\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.test.ts:12:  it(\"fakeModelCallText resolves to ok + text\", async () => {\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.test.ts:13:    const result = await fakeModelCallText(\"hello\")(INPUT);\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.test.ts:17:  it(\"fakeModelCallFailure resolves to each classified failure kind\", async () => {\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.test.ts:27:      const result = await fakeModelCallFailure(kind)(INPUT);\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.test.ts:33:  it(\"fakeModelCallRouter routes by provider/model and fails closed on an unrouted key\", async () => {\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.test.ts:34:    const router = fakeModelCallRouter({\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.test.ts:35:      \"openai-codex/gpt-5.4\": fakeModelCallText(\"from openai\"),\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.test.ts:36:      \"anthropic/claude-3\": fakeModelCallText(\"from anthropic\"),\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/assignments.ts:2:// missing/unknown/placeholder.\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/assignments.ts:38:  readonly problem: \"incomplete\" | \"unknown_prompt\" | \"placeholder\";\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/assignments.ts:40:  constructor(kind: FormKind, problem: \"incomplete\" | \"unknown_prompt\" | \"placeholder\") {\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/assignments.ts:49:      case \"placeholder\":\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/assignments.ts:50:        message = `Assignment for '${kind}' contains placeholder values: provider and model must be concrete identifiers`;\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/assignments.ts:69:/** Check if an assignment contains placeholder values that would mask misconfiguration. */\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/assignments.ts:71:  const placeholderPatterns = [\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/assignments.ts:73:    \"placeholder\",\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/assignments.ts:83:  return placeholderPatterns.some((pattern) =>\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/assignments.ts:99:  // Check for placeholder values (AC-5.6: placeholder fails loud)\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/assignments.ts:101:    throw new AssignmentValidationError(kind, \"placeholder\");\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/assignments.ts:123: *  as an actionable diagnostic. It never returns an incomplete or placeholder map\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/assignments.ts:145:      throw new Error(`Unknown assignment key '${key}': must be one of ${FORM_KINDS.join(\", \")}`);\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.test.ts:13:describe(\"corpus fixtures\", () => {\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.test.ts:23:      // a real recorded sequence, not an empty stub, and a closed turn\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:3:**Status:** Draft for review. **Ready for tech design once M0 lands.** Per the PRD's M0 gate, this epic's capture-verification tests consume the M0 corpus fixtures (Story 3) and two M0 decisions must be settled before tech design: the image/file-reference handling choice and the tool-call rendering choice (A5). These are named as M0 inputs below, not left as open tech design questions.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:27:**M0** is the pre-epic period that produces the recorded PI **corpora** (real sessions captured to fixtures) and the **converter** prototype (PI events → intake events). Live recon (June 2026) already established the PI event shapes this epic depends on; M0's remaining work is corpus breadth, the productionized converter, and derivation dial-in. This epic's capture-verification tests consume M0 corpora as fixtures.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:92:| A-2 | M0 delivers recorded PI corpora as test fixtures and a converter prototype to productionize. | Pending (M0) | The epic spec stands, but the build and the verification flow's tests wait on M0 corpora. Capture ACs are spec-complete; their fixtures are an M0 input. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:96:| A-6 | LHC `turn_end` with no open turn is a contractual no-op (Epic 01), tolerating PI's pre-first-prompt and dangling events. | Validated (LHC v1 spec) | If not a no-op, the converter would need to guard turn closes. Confirmed specced behavior. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:97:| A-7 | The image / file-reference handling decision (placeholder vs. extending the intake payload schema) is settled by M0 before this epic's tech design. | Pending (M0 gate) | The converter's content-part mapping (Flow 2) depends on it. Recon found `@file.png` stays literal text in print mode; the interactive paste shape is uncaptured. M0 corpora establish frequency; the decision is an M0 output, not a tech design question. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:175:**AC-2.6:** Capture is duplicate-safe. Re-delivered events (reload, crash-replay) are recognized by idempotency key and skipped rather than recorded twice. On normal resume PI re-delivers no historical events, so duplication only arises on the reload and replay paths.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:223:**AC-5.5:** A user override — a different provider, model, or prompt for any kind — takes effect on the next session start with no code change. An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error, never a silent skip or a placeholder default that masks the misconfiguration.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:229:Capture is verified without serving anything to a model. A recorded PI corpus replays through the converter; the resulting thread's read-back is matched against the fixture's expectation. Replay is deterministic: the same corpus yields the same thread every time. Inspect surfaces report the captured session — event and message counts, last recorded position, and any gaps.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:231:**AC-6.1:** Replaying a recorded PI corpus through the converter produces a thread whose read-back — events, messages, and turns — matches the fixture's expectation, including the chatty, tool-heavy, parallel-tool, error-result, and aborted-turn corpora.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:281:Image and file-reference content parts: LHC intake payloads are text today. Handling (placeholder vs. payload-schema extension) is an M0 decision (A-7), not a tech design question; until that decision lands, the converter must not silently drop a content part without recording the omission.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:326:| Hard kill | user `message_end` only; no assistant close, no `turn_end`, no `agent_end` | On reattach, find a dangling open turn with a trailing user prompt; tolerate it (LHC `turn_end`-with-no-open-turn is a no-op); resume capture without duplicating the recorded prefix |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:330:Each emitted event carries a stable idempotency key so re-delivery (reload, crash-replay) is recognized and skipped. Construction precedence (from the POC mapper, carried forward): PI entry id (`pi:<session>:entry:<entryId>`) → provider response id / tool-call id → content fingerprint as last resort. The exact construction is tech design; the contract is that the key is stable across re-delivery of the same logical event and unique across distinct events.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:376:`FormKind` is the seven derivation kinds: `smoothed_prompt`, `tool_call_summary`, `tool_result_summary`, `turn_rendering`, `lower_band_projection`, `chunk_summary_detailed`, `chunk_summary_brief`. `prompt` must name a registered prompt. Initialization fails loudly if any kind is missing, references an unknown prompt, or carries a placeholder.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:391:- **Fail loud on misconfiguration.** Missing, unknown, or placeholder assignments fail at initialization with an actionable error, not a silent skip.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:414:**Delivers:** The walking skeleton: extension package scaffold, the PI extension entry point and hook registration rail, the plain-data-only state holder, the LHC instance init/dispose seam (stubbed), test fixture and corpus-loading harness, and typed fail-closed stubs.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:417:**Boundary / risk notes:** Stubs fail closed, never fake success (tech arch convention). The plain-data-only rule (AC-1.3) is established here as the state-holder shape so no later story is tempted to cache a context object.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:418:**Flows/ACs covered:** None directly; smoke tests only — extension loads, registers hooks, init/dispose seam runs, fixture loader produces valid intake-event shapes.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:441:**Delivers:** Corpus replay through the converter, read-back matching against fixtures, deterministic re-replay, and inspect overview/health reflecting the captured session.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:443:**Prerequisite:** Story 2. Consumes M0 corpora as fixtures.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:444:**Boundary / risk notes:** This story's fixtures are the M0 corpora (A-2). If corpora are not yet broad, the story builds the replay-equality and inspect machinery against whatever corpora exist and widens as M0 delivers.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:543:- **TC-2.7** — A re-delivered event (reload/replay) is skipped by idempotency key; a normal resume re-delivers no historical events.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:559:- **TC-5.6** — An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error; no placeholder default masks it.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md:560:- **TC-6.1** — Each corpus (chatty, tool-heavy, parallel-tool, error-result, aborted-turn) replays to a thread whose read-back matches the fixture.\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/thread.ts:3:// so the store is never mocked). Mirrors the lhc package's `tempStore` so the\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/thread.ts:51:    throw new Error(`temp thread creation failed: ${created.error.reason}`);\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/synthetic.test.ts:11:} from \"./synthetic.js\";\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/synthetic.test.ts:13:describe(\"synthetic builders\", () => {\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/03-capture-verification.md:24:- Creating M0 corpora. This story consumes M0 corpora as fixtures.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/03-capture-verification.md:28:**Dependencies:** Story 2. M0 corpora are fixture inputs.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/03-capture-verification.md:33:**AC-6.1:** Replaying a recorded PI corpus through the converter produces a thread whose read-back — events, messages, and turns — matches the fixture's expectation, including the chatty, tool-heavy, parallel-tool, error-result, and aborted-turn corpora.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/03-capture-verification.md:35:**TC-6.1** — Each corpus (chatty, tool-heavy, parallel-tool, error-result, aborted-turn) replays to a thread whose read-back matches the fixture.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/03-capture-verification.md:59:- The implementation is narrow, but fixture fidelity and deterministic replay are easy to fake with shallow comparisons.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/03-capture-verification.md:63:- M0 corpora breadth determines final fixture coverage; the replay machinery should widen as corpora arrive.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/03-capture-verification.md:71:| Corpus fixtures | `packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts` |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/03-capture-verification.md:72:| Temp threads | `packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/thread.ts` |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/03-capture-verification.md:73:| Verification tests | `packages/pi-lhc/test/verify/replay.test.ts`, `packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.test.ts` |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/03-capture-verification.md:91:| TC-6.1 | `test/verify/replay.test.ts` | Each available recorded corpus replays through the converter to a thread whose events, messages, and turns match fixture expectation. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/03-capture-verification.md:99:| Fixture validity | `test/fixtures/corpus.test.ts` | Corpus loader yields valid `MessageEventInput` shapes and lifecycle-coherent sequences. | Replay tests can pass against bad fixtures unless the fixture substrate is checked independently. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/03-capture-verification.md:120:- Targeted: `pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify -- test/verify/replay.test.ts test/fixtures/corpus.test.ts`\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.ts:1:// Deterministic ModelCall fakes — the inference edge (tech design §Fixture\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.ts:8:export function fakeModelCallText(text: string): ModelCall {\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.ts:13:export function fakeModelCallFailure(\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.ts:20:/** Routes each call by its `provider/model` key to a per-lane fake — the\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.ts:23:export function fakeModelCallRouter(\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.ts:34:        message: `no fake route for ${key}`,\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:59:**AC-2.6:** Capture is duplicate-safe. Re-delivered events (reload, crash-replay) are recognized by idempotency key and skipped rather than recorded twice. On normal resume PI re-delivers no historical events, so duplication only arises on the reload and replay paths.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:61:**TC-2.7** — A re-delivered event (reload/replay) is skipped by idempotency key; a normal resume re-delivers no historical events.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:126:| TC-2.7 | `test/capture/parallel-and-errors.test.ts` | Re-delivered event is skipped by idempotency key; normal resume does not replay historical messages. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:134:| Idempotency / Retry | `test/capture/idempotency.test.ts` | Replaying the same corpus twice produces no duplicate events; re-delivered keys return `skipped`. | Single-event dedup does not prove reload/replay safety across a corpus. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:154:- Evidence: capture tests assert stored events, duplicate skips, no hook exception, and exact turn boundaries through the real LHC intake path.\npackages/pi-lhc/src/shared/not-implemented.ts:3:// The two fail-closed primitives every Epic 1 stub uses until its owning story\npackages/pi-lhc/src/shared/not-implemented.ts:4:// lands. The anti-shim rule (story §Anti-Shim Requirements): no stub may report\npackages/pi-lhc/src/shared/not-implemented.ts:6:// replay. Pure algorithms throw `NotImplementedError`; services/adapters return\npackages/pi-lhc/src/shared/not-implemented.ts:10:/** Thrown by pure-algorithm stubs (map-message, idempotency, turn-accumulator,\npackages/pi-lhc/src/shared/not-implemented.ts:12: *  never that this was thrown (test-plan: \"never asserting on NotImplementedError\"). */\npackages/pi-lhc/src/shared/not-implemented.ts:13:export class NotImplementedError extends Error {\npackages/pi-lhc/src/shared/not-implemented.ts:16:    super(`${operation} is not implemented yet (Epic 1 foundation stub)`);\npackages/pi-lhc/src/shared/not-implemented.ts:17:    this.name = \"NotImplementedError\";\npackages/pi-lhc/src/shared/not-implemented.ts:22:/** Fail-closed structured result for service/adapter/handler stubs. Uses LHC's\npackages/pi-lhc/src/shared/not-implemented.ts:30:      reason: `${operation} is not implemented yet (Epic 1 foundation stub)`,\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:3:// delivers real recordings, the loaders build synthetic stand-ins; the\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:15:} from \"./synthetic.js\";\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:6:Build the walking skeleton for the pi-lhc extension: package scaffold, PI hook registration rail, plain-data-only state holder, stubbed LHC init/dispose seam, corpus fixture loader, and typed fail-closed stubs.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:13:**Objective:** Establish the loadable extension structure every later story builds on, with no stale PI context retained across hook boundaries and no stub that can fake successful capture.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:20:- Test fixture and corpus-loading harness that can load recorded PI corpora and expected intake-event shapes.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:21:- Typed fail-closed stubs for unimplemented capture, inference, validation, and fork behavior.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:41:- Fixture loader reads recorded corpus fixtures and produces structurally valid intake-event expectations.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:50:No Epic 1 product AC is completed here. The story proves that the extension can be loaded, that future handlers have fail-closed seams, and that fixture/test utilities exist before behavior stories depend on them.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:57:- This is foundation work with no AC owner, but later stories can be reward-hacked if stubs fake success.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:58:- Red should prove fixture invariants, package gates, and load/registration smoke before Green fills the scaffold.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:71:| Capture/inference/fork stubs | `packages/pi-lhc/src/capture/*`, `packages/pi-lhc/src/inference/*`, `packages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/fork.ts` |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:72:| Fixtures | `packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts`, `packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/synthetic.ts`, `packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.ts`, `packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/thread.ts` |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:87:| None | `test/fixtures/corpus.test.ts`, extension-load smoke, package script checks | Foundation has no story-owned TC; smoke checks prove load, hook rail, fail-closed stubs, fixture validity, and verification-script availability. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:93:| Fixture validity | `test/fixtures/corpus.test.ts` | Corpus loader yields valid `MessageEventInput` shapes and lifecycle-coherent sequences; named invalid builders produce intended malformed shapes. | Story 0 owns no AC/TC, but later capture tests depend on trustworthy fixtures. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:99:- Keep `state.ts` complete rather than stubbed: it is the shape that prevents stale PI context retention later.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:103:- No stub may report successful capture, derivation, validation, fork seeding, or replay before the owning story implements it.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:104:- Fixture loaders must parse and validate shape; do not hardcode passing fixture counts.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:130:- Smoke tests cover load, hook registration, plain-data state, init/dispose seam, and fixture loading.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/coverage.md:89:| Story 3: Capture Verification | fixture / packaging + capstone integration | Capture correctness is provable from recorded corpora without serving anything to a model. | Fixture dependency | M0 corpora breadth determines final fixture coverage | Keep separate because verification is the proof layer for capture, not more converter behavior |\npackages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/instance.ts:69: *  shutdown with no live instance is a successful no-op, never an error. */\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/05-inference-host-routing.md:82:| Deterministic fakes | `packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.ts` |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/05-inference-host-routing.md:117:- TC coverage uses deterministic fakes, so auth availability never skips tests.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/05-inference-host-routing.md:122:- Do not return placeholder text for failed derivations; return classified failures.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:31:| I-6 | Deviation | Epic A-7 names image/file-reference handling an M0 decision. | **Design position pending M0, with a safe fallback specified.** The current LHC intake schema has no image/file payload kind beyond text/runtime-note material. Until M0 chooses placeholder vs. schema extension, unsupported content parts degrade to a `runtime_note` event recording the omission (never silent drop). If M0 extends the intake schema, the converter gains kinds; the degrade-to-`runtime_note` path remains the fallback for unsupported parts. This unblocks implementation planning without pre-empting the M0 call. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:84:The boundaries that matter for testing are the two edges the connector does not own: the **PI hook surface** (events arrive; the design feeds them from recorded corpora and synthetic builders) and the **`ModelCall` function** as it reaches PI's `modelRegistry` + `complete` (the design supplies a deterministic fake). Everything inside the LHC box runs real against a temp SQLite file, because persistence, reattach, replay, and fork are the product contract — mocking the store would prove nothing about the behaviors this epic exists to guarantee.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:167:    fixtures/\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:168:      corpus.ts                    NEW  recorded-corpus loader (M0 fixtures)\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:169:      synthetic.ts                 NEW  synthetic AgentMessage / event builders\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:170:      model-call.ts                NEW  deterministic ModelCall fakes (text / each failure kind)\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:174:The two mock boundaries are explicit in the tree: `test/fixtures/corpus.ts` + `synthetic.ts` feed the PI hook edge, and `test/fixtures/model-call.ts` supplies the inference edge. No module under `src/` is mocked by another module under `src/` — the converter is tested through the capture entry point against a real temp thread, not in isolation with a mocked intake.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:194:| `inference/assignments.ts` | NEW | Load seven assignments from config; fail loud on missing/unknown/placeholder; override on next start | — | AC-5.4, AC-5.5 |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:270:TC approaches (TC-1.1…TC-1.5) live in `test-plan.md`; each asserts on observable thread/mapping state through a real temp thread, with the lifecycle entry point driven by synthetic `session_start`/`shutdown` events. The reattach test (TC-1.5) reconstructs from the durable record after discarding the in-memory holder — the architecture-risk test for restart survival.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:324:| Map one message | `capture/map-message.ts` | `mapMessage(msg: AgentMessage, ctx: MapCtx): MessageEventInput[]` | pure; `NotImplementedError` until Green |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:329:The idempotency key gets real depth because dedup correctness (AC-2.6) and crash-replay both depend on it. Construction precedence, carried from the POC and adapted to the SDK's per-event-key field: PI entry id when present (`pi:<session>:<entryId>:<block>:<kind>`), else provider `responseId` (assistant) or `toolCallId` (tool result), else a `role:timestamp:content-hash` fallback. The contract is that the key is identical across re-delivery of the same logical event and distinct across different events; the SDK's `messageEvents` returns `skipped` with `skipReason: duplicate_idempotency_key` for a re-delivered key, which is the dedup mechanism the test asserts against.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:401:| Load + shape-validate | `inference/assignments.ts` | `loadAssignments(config): Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>` | throws on missing/unknown/placeholder |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:407:Because the epic is observe-only, capture correctness is proven without serving anything to a model. `verify/replay.ts` replays a recorded corpus through the converter into a temp thread and compares the resulting read-back — events, messages, turns — against the fixture's expectation. The same corpus replayed twice yields identical read-back, because the converter's mapping and the SDK's deterministic IDs make replay reproducible (AC-6.2). The corpora are the M0 fixtures (chatty, tool-heavy, parallel-tool, error-result, aborted-turn); until M0 delivers breadth, the machinery builds against whatever corpora exist and widens as they arrive. inspect's overview and health reads report event/message counts, last recorded position, and any capture gaps or failed derivations (AC-6.3).\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:419:These are copy-paste targets for the foundation chunk. Types reference the ACs they serve; signatures are complete (no `any`, no TODO). The LHC-side types (`MessageEventInput`, `ModelCall`, `ModelAssignment`, `FormKind`, `ThreadRef`, `OpResult`) are imported from the `lhc` package, not redefined — the connector consumes them.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:499:): Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>; // throws on missing/unknown/placeholder\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:542:| Idempotency / Retry | `capture/idempotency.test.ts` | Replaying the same corpus twice produces no duplicate events; re-delivered keys come back `skipped` | TC checks dedup once; the hazard is re-delivery under reload/replay being safe |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:546:| Fixture Validity | `test/fixtures/corpus.test.ts` | Corpus loader yields valid `MessageEventInput` shapes and lifecycle-coherent sequences | Fixture correctness is test substrate, not product behavior, so no AC covers it |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:557:The epic's verification flow and most capture tests run on fixtures, so fixture validity is a foundation invariant. Two fixture families:\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:559:- **Recorded corpora** (`test/fixtures/corpus.ts`) — the M0 captures, loaded and validated to produce well-formed `MessageEventInput` sequences. Each corpus is a named lifecycle (chatty, tool-heavy, parallel-tool, error-result, aborted-turn). Default loaders represent valid captures; an explicitly named `makeTruncatedCorpus()` produces a deliberately malformed sequence for the capture-failure test.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:560:- **Synthetic builders** (`test/fixtures/synthetic.ts`) — `makeUserMessage`, `makeAssistantMessage({ thinking?, text?, toolCalls? })`, `makeToolResult({ id, isError? })`, and event-stream builders for `session_start`/`agent_end` etc. These drive the lifecycle and turn-derivation tests where a precise hand-built shape beats a recorded one.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:561:- **Deterministic ModelCall fakes** (`test/fixtures/model-call.ts`) — `fakeModelCallText(text)`, `fakeModelCallFailure(kind)`, and a per-kind router for multi-lane tests.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:577:- **Turn derivation** — exact inputs (a PI event sequence) to exact outputs (the ordered LHC events plus the single `turn_end`). The worked example in Flow 2 is the first golden case; others cover an aborted run (turn closes complete-but-aborted at `agent_end` with partial content) and a hard-kill (no `agent_end` → open turn left open, tolerated as a no-op on reattach).\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:586:The test architecture follows directly from where this component's real boundaries are. The connector's own modules — converter, mapper, accumulator, model-call, validation — are never mocked against each other; they are exercised through their entry points (the hook handlers, the converter, the replay harness) so wiring bugs between them surface. Mocks live only at the two true external edges.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:593:    /--------\\  driven by synthetic events + corpora, real temp SQLite\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:595:  /------------\\ classifyFailure — pure, no mocks\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:599:**The critical mocking rule, applied:** mock at the external boundary, never at internal module boundaries. The external boundaries are the PI hook surface (fed by synthetic events and recorded corpora) and the `ModelCall`'s reach into PI's `modelRegistry` + pi-ai `complete` (supplied as a deterministic fake). Everything LHC runs real.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:603:| PI hook events | Fed, not mocked | Synthetic builders + recorded corpora are the input, not a mocked dependency |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:604:| `modelRegistry` + pi-ai `complete` | Yes (the fake `ModelCall`) | External boundary — control completion text and failure shapes |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:610:**Stateful local persistence:** reattach (TC-1.5), replay-equality (TC-6.2), and fork source-immutability (TC-3.1) all use real temp thread directories and prove behavior across a reopen or a second thread, because mocking the store would make these tests assert nothing.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:612:The verification tiers are the `lhc` package's configured commands, which `pi-lhc` mirrors (pnpm workspace, vitest, tsc). What gets mocked appears again here intentionally; the same boundary is stated in External Contracts and in the matrix.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:637:Archetypes: **Pure Type Foundation** (the interfaces above), **Fixture/Test Foundation** (corpus loader, synthetic builders, ModelCall fakes, temp-thread factory), **Orchestration/Command Foundation** (the extension entry point and hook-registration rail with structured-result stubs), and **Verification Gate Foundation** (package scripts/config for the gates named here).\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:642:| Extension shell | `src/index.ts` | Hook registration; routes to stubbed handlers; holds no state |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:644:| Fixtures | `test/fixtures/{corpus,synthetic,model-call}.ts` | Valid-default builders + named invalid builders |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:645:| Temp-thread factory | `test/fixtures/thread.ts` | Real temp SQLite thread per test |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:648:**Exit criteria:** package verification scripts exist and run; `red-verify` passes; fixture-invariant tests pass (corpus shapes valid, synthetic builders produce coherent sequences, temp-thread factory creates and reopens a thread); the extension loads and registers hooks (smoke).\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:660:**Scope:** corpus replay, read-back compare, deterministic re-replay, inspect surfacing. **ACs:** 6.1–6.3. **Relevant sections:** Flow 6, `verify/replay.ts`, Fixture Validity risk. **Substrate maturity:** consumes Chunk 2's converter through the real intake path, not a seam; consumes M0 corpora as fixtures (widens as M0 delivers).\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:668:**Scope:** the `ModelCall`, multi-lane routing, classification, and the closed-loop proof. **ACs:** 4.1–4.5. **Relevant sections:** Flow 4, `inference/model-call.ts`, Adapter-Boundary + Concurrency risks. **Substrate maturity:** AC-4.5 consumes Chunk 2's captured content through the real scheduler + `drainSettled`, not a fake derivation. **Architecture-risk tests:** failure-shape→kind mapping, stale-result no-clobber.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:694:| Q3 | Final breadth of M0 corpora for Chunk 3 fixtures | M0 / Lee | Chunk 3 coverage | Pending; machinery builds against available corpora |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:51:**AC-5.5:** A user override — a different provider, model, or prompt for any kind — takes effect on the next session start with no code change. An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error, never a silent skip or a placeholder default that masks the misconfiguration.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:55:**TC-5.6** — An incomplete or unknown assignment fails loudly at initialization with an actionable error; no placeholder default masks it.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:107:| TC-5.6 | `test/inference/assignments.test.ts` | Missing kind, unknown prompt, incomplete assignment, or placeholder fails loudly at initialization. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:122:- Do not skip validation because auth is unavailable; deterministic fakes must exercise the not-logged-in path.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/06-startup-validation-and-assignment-config.md:147:- Missing, unknown, incomplete, or placeholder assignments fail loudly.\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:8:// These tests use synthetic PI contexts (mock-free at the b