{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba packages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/thread-resolution.ts | sed -n '108,148p' && nl -ba packages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts | sed -n '210,227p'\"","aggregated_output":"   108\t/** Reload reconstruction (AC-1.5): on reload — the extension torn down and\n   109\t *  re-initialized while the same PI session continues — re-resolve the session's\n   110\t *  thread from DURABLE registry state. It reads no retained in-process memory and\n   111\t *  never creates a new thread or re-prompts a picker; the resolved id comes back\n   112\t *  out of the registry (the durable catalog), so reconstruction survives the loss\n   113\t *  of every in-memory holder (the architecture-risk scenario).\n   114\t *\n   115\t *  - `--session <id>` / `--continue`: re-resolve by the same durable launch input.\n   116\t *    Both are idempotent — resolving twice returns the same thread and creates\n   117\t *    nothing — so the normal resolver already reattaches correctly.\n   118\t *  - no flag or `--resume`: reattach to the cwd's most-recently-created thread,\n   119\t *    read from the registry. A no-flag Story-1 session created exactly one thread\n   120\t *    in its cwd, so that is the thread; re-running the launch instead would create\n   121\t *    a duplicate (no flag) or re-prompt the operator (`--resume`).\n   122\t *\n   123\t *  Returns `null` when the cwd has no thread to reattach (a reload against an\n   124\t *  empty registry); the caller records a diagnostic rather than creating one.\n   125\t *\n   126\t *  This is the answer to \"where does the durable reload id live without a\n   127\t *  PI-session→thread mapping record\" (epic I-1): it lives in the LHC registry,\n   128\t *  the system of record, not in a connector field or a recovery sidecar. The\n   129\t *  cwd-most-recent reattach is an Epic-1 convenience; the permanent home for\n   130\t *  reload/fork lineage is LHC thread metadata (Feature 2+). */\n   131\texport async function resolveReloadThread(\n   132\t  launch: LaunchFlags,\n   133\t  deps: ResolveDeps,\n   134\t): Promise<OpResult<ThreadRef | null>> {\n   135\t  if (launch.session !== undefined) {\n   136\t    return resolveThread({ session: launch.session }, deps);\n   137\t  }\n   138\t  if (launch.continue === true) {\n   139\t    return resolveThread({ continue: true }, deps);\n   140\t  }\n   141\t  const listed = await threads.listThreads({ cwd: deps.cwd, ...registryArg(deps.registryPath) });\n   142\t  if (!listed.ok) return listed;\n   143\t  // listThreads orders by created_at then rowid, so the last cwd-scoped row is\n   144\t  // the most recently created thread for this directory.\n   145\t  const mostRecent = listed.value.at(-1);\n   146\t  if (mostRecent === undefined) return { ok: true, value: null };\n   147\t  return { ok: true, value: threadRefById(mostRecent.threadId, deps.registryPath) };\n   148\t}\n   210\t/** Default `--resume` selection. PI v0.79.2 exposes `ctx.ui.notify` but no\n   211\t *  interactive input/selection surface (wiring research), so the production\n   212\t *  picker presents the cwd-scoped candidate list — title + creation time — to the\n   213\t *  operator through `ctx.ui.notify` when a UI is present and only auto-resolves\n   214\t *  the single unambiguous candidate. Multiple candidates fail closed with\n   215\t *  `null`, directing the operator to relaunch with `--session <id>`; this avoids\n   216\t *  silently attaching capture to the wrong thread. The picker itself stays\n   217\t *  cwd-scoped, titled, and selection-injectable (`pickThread` takes the `select`\n   218\t *  seam); tests drive an explicit operator choice through it. */\n   219\tfunction defaultSelectThread(\n   220\t  choices: readonly ThreadChoice[],\n   221\t  ctx: ExtensionContext,\n   222\t): Promise<string | null> {\n   223\t  if (ctx.hasUI) {\n   224\t    ctx.ui.notify(renderResumeCandidates(choices), { level: choices.length === 1 ? \"info\" : \"warn\" });\n   225\t  }\n   226\t  return Promise.resolve(choices.length === 1 ? choices[0]?.threadId ?? null : null);\n   227\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"outcome\":\"block\",\"crossStoryFindings\":[\"Stories 2, 4, 5, and 6 are mostly coherent: capture, turn derivation, fork replay, inference routing, and startup validation all run through connector entry points and real LHC surfaces.\",\"Story 1 thread identity drift remains material: the current code both adds a PI custom-entry thread pointer and still keeps a cwd-most-recent fallback, while the epic and tech design describe launch/registry resolution with no PI-session-to-thread mapping.\",\"Story 3 does not meet the epic-level verification contract because the corpus loaders are synthetic stand-ins rather than recorded PI corpora.\"],\"architectureFindings\":[\"Epic 1 correctly remains observe-only: index.ts registers no context hook and capture does not alter what PI serves to the model.\",\"The converter architecture matches the tech design: map-message is pure, turn-accumulator owns the LHC turn boundary, and converter writes through intakeStream.messageEvents.\",\"The current reload implementation conflicts with the tech design resolution of I-1/I-7: tech-design.md says there is no stored PI-session-to-thread pointer, but index.ts writes and reads a pi-lhc-thread-state custom entry via appendEntry/sessionManager.\",\"The production --resume default does not resolve an operator selection. It notifies the candidate list, auto-resolves only one candidate, and returns null for multiple candidates.\",\"Fork seeding uses the real intake path and source read-back is not written, but the fork path uses a filePath ThreadRef for the target, which is another sign that lifecycle thread identity is not consistently registry-id based.\"],\"epicCoverageAssessment\":[\"Flow 1 is incomplete against the written architecture and AC-1.7: launch resolution exists, but reload identity and resume selection still require human ruling or design update.\",\"Flow 2 is covered by source and tests: ordered message mapping, turn derivation, runtime notes, idempotency, gaps, parallel tools, errors, and aborts are implemented.\",\"Flow 3 is implemented with replay seeding and derived-form requeue; source immutability is covered.\",\"Flow 4 is implemented through createModelCall over PI modelRegistry plus pi-ai dynamic import, with classified failures and closed-loop persisted outcomes covered.\",\"Flow 5 is implemented: assignments load defaults/overrides, startup validation reports unreachable lanes, and capture continues.\",\"Flow 6 is not fully covered: replay is deterministic and compares real read-back, but it consumes synthetic fixtures instead of recorded PI corpora required by AC-6.1.\"],\"productionPathFindings\":[\"No fake adapter was found in src capture/fork/inspect paths: capture uses real LHC intakeStream, fork replay uses intakeStream.messageEvents, and inspect reads real LHC surfaces.\",\"Test-only model fakes are confined to test/fixtures and are not production code.\",\"createModelCall does not fabricate output when pi-ai is missing; it returns a classified failure.\",\"Material non-real verification path: packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts explicitly says loaders build synthetic stand-ins until M0 delivers recordings, so Flow 6 is not proving recorded PI corpus replay.\",\"Material architecture drift: production index.ts persists threadRef in PI custom entries, despite tech-design.md stating the mapping persistence problem is dissolved and no session-file pointer is written.\",\"Material resume limitation: production defaultSelectThread has no interactive operator selection path for multiple cwd-scoped threads.\"],\"blockingFindings\":[{\"id\":\"EV-001\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"Capture verification uses synthetic stand-ins instead of recorded PI corpora\",\"evidence\":\"epic.md:69 and epic.md:229-231 require recorded corpora replay through the converter. packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:1-5 states the loaders build synthetic stand-ins until M0 delivers real recordings; loadAllCorpora at lines 137-149 returns only those synthetic loaders.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md\",\"packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts\",\"packages/pi-lhc/src/verify/replay.ts\",\"packages/pi-lhc/test/verify/replay.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-6.1\",\"TC-6.1\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"fresh-fix-path\",\"blocking\":true},{\"id\":\"EV-002\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"Production --resume does not resolve an operator selection\",\"evidence\":\"AC-1.7 in epic.md:141 requires --resume to resolve the operator's selection. index.ts:210-226 only notifies the candidate list and returns the single candidate or null for multiple candidates; thread-resolution.test.ts:255-292 asserts fail-closed behavior for multiple candidates rather than a selected thread.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts\",\"packages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/picker.ts\",\"packages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-1.7\",\"TC-1.7\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"human-ruling\",\"blocking\":true},{\"id\":\"EV-003\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"Reload identity reintroduced a PI custom-entry mapping contrary to the tech design\",\"evidence\":\"tech-design.md:22 says there is no PI-session-to-thread mapping to persist, and tech-design.md:26 says appendEntry is moot for mapping because no session-file pointer is written. index.ts:134-158 defines pi-lhc-thread-state, index.ts:304-306 reads it from ctx.sessionManager.getEntries(), index.ts:468-469 appends the current threadRef, and index.ts:677-679 wires that append through pi.appendEntry.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md\",\"packages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts\",\"packages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-1.5\",\"TC-1.5\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"human-ruling\",\"blocking\":true}],\"nonBlockingFindings\":[{\"id\":\"NF-001\",\"severity\":\"observation\",\"title\":\"Configured gates pass\",\"evidence\":\"Ran pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify and pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify-all; both completed successfully with 20 test files and 143 tests passing.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/pi-lhc/package.json\"],\"requirementIds\":[],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"same-session-implementor\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NF-002\",\"severity\":\"observation\",\"title\":\"Local PI and pi-ai type declarations are temporary boundaries\",\"evidence\":\"packages/pi-lhc/src/pi/types.ts declares the consumed PI API slice because the real package is not a workspace dependency; packages/pi-lhc/src/inference/pi-ai.ts provides local pi-ai typing while model-call dynamically imports @earendil-works/pi-ai at runtime.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/pi-lhc/src/pi/types.ts\",\"packages/pi-lhc/src/inference/pi-ai.ts\",\"packages/pi-lhc/src/inference/model-call.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-4.1\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"fresh-fix-path\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NF-003\",\"severity\":\"observation\",\"title\":\"Derived-form reuse remains deferred but correctness is preserved\",\"evidence\":\"fork.ts seeds forked threads by replay and does not copy ready derived forms; AC-3.3 permits requeue when reuse safety cannot be proven.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/fork.ts\",\"packages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/fork.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-3.3\",\"TC-3.3\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"fresh-fix-path\",\"blocking\":false}],\"unresolvedItems\":[\"Add real recorded PI corpora or formally narrow AC-6.1 before passing the epic.\",\"Human ruling or tech-design update needed for the PI custom-entry threadRef mapping introduced in index.ts.\",\"Human ruling or PI API change needed for --resume operator selection in production.\",\"If the custom-entry mapping is accepted, update the architecture docs and lifecycle tests so thread identity is consistently specified as registry threadId, including fork reload.\"],\"gateResult\":\"pass\"}"}}
